You're supposed to be in Jakarta.

490
00:40:25.348 --> 00:40:28.309
I had a conference in LA.
Oh, my word. And here I am!

491
00:40:28.393 --> 00:40:30.270
-Here she is!
-My beauty.

492
00:40:31.229 --> 00:40:32.230
Uh...

493
00:40:32.313 --> 00:40:34.607
Uh... Mom.

494
00:40:35.483 --> 00:40:38.319
-Uh... This is, uh, Charlotte.
-Charlotte.

495
00:40:38.403 --> 00:40:40.781
Hello. Such a pleasure to meet you, Mrs.--

496
00:40:40.865 --> 00:40:43.909
Ann. Just Ann, honey.
It's nice to meet you, too.

497
00:40:48.413 --> 00:40:50.874
-Charlotte, some peas, please.
-Mm-hm.

498
00:40:51.541 --> 00:40:53.585
Barry, honey, do you have a salad bowl?

499
00:41:02.219 --> 00:41:05.680
-Does this work?
-How about a knife?

500
00:41:11.519 --> 00:41:12.813
How about this?

501
00:41:12.897 --> 00:41:17.151
-Not exactly Julia Child, huh?
-Well, Saleem's sort of a child.

502
00:41:17.234 --> 00:41:19.820
Oh, yeah?
Well, you're sort of a poopy-head.

503
00:41:20.737 --> 00:41:23.448
Is the atmosphere around here
always so intellectually charged?

504
00:41:23.531 --> 00:41:25.993
-Oh, yeah.
-Well, you know, as a feminist,

505
00:41:26.076 --> 00:41:29.789
I would never endorse this sort
of retrograde patriarchal paradigm

506
00:41:29.872 --> 00:41:32.958
that would paint you as a sort
of domesticating, civilizing presence

507
00:41:33.042 --> 00:41:35.252
-in this household.
-Oh, no. Of course not.

508
00:41:35.335 --> 00:41:38.088
But if I did, I would say
that you have got your hands full

509
00:41:38.172 --> 00:41:39.131
with these two knuckleheads.

510
00:41:39.215 --> 00:41:41.382
-Damn, Barry.

511
00:41:41.466 --> 00:41:43.551
Your mom can talk some shit.

512
00:41:45.179 --> 00:41:47.807
So, do we get to hear
some embarrassing stories

513
00:41:47.889 --> 00:41:48.973
about Barry or what?

514
00:41:50.433 --> 00:41:52.769
I'm sorry to be dull,
but I don't really have any.

515
00:41:52.853 --> 00:41:55.189
He was so easy.
Even his birth was easy.

516
00:41:55.272 --> 00:41:58.608
I was 19 at that time,
so that wasn't the easiest, and...

517
00:41:59.484 --> 00:42:02.154
Neither was his dad, but what can I say?

518
00:42:02.237 --> 00:42:03.488
He's my darling boy.

519
00:42:04.405 --> 00:42:06.491
What wasn't easy about his dad?

520
00:42:06.741 --> 00:42:08.409
Barry barely talks about him.

521
00:42:08.493 --> 00:42:12.664
All I know is that he's some kind
of high-up government official.

522
00:42:13.581 --> 00:42:14.666
I only met him once.

523
00:42:15.625 --> 00:42:17.962
He showed up in Hawaii
when I was ten and...

524
00:42:18.044 --> 00:42:19.587
tried to tell me how to live my life.

525
00:42:20.380 --> 00:42:22.883
That's not true.
He was around when you were little.

526
00:42:24.509 --> 00:42:27.012
He's a lovely man. We just
weren't well-suited for each other.

527
00:42:27.096 --> 00:42:28.889
-Hmm.
- Hmm.

528
00:42:28.973 --> 00:42:33.102
Mm, 'cause he had another family
in Kenya, Mom. He lied to you.

529
00:42:33.185 --> 00:42:36.396
It's a little more complicated than that.
But we don't have to talk about it.

530
00:42:36.479 --> 00:42:39.149
Okay. She always defends him.

531
00:42:39.232 --> 00:42:39.899
You know what?

532
00:42:40.900 --> 00:42:42.110
He gave me you.

533
00:42:45.530 --> 00:42:48.200
But Barry must've had
some terrible haircuts, at least.

534
00:42:48.284 --> 00:42:50.827
This is true. I used to cut his hair.

535
00:42:50.911 --> 00:42:54.290
Mm. That's something
no white woman should do.

536
00:42:56.876 --> 00:42:58.085
Probably not.

537
00:43:03.423 --> 00:43:05.925
Your mom is awesome.

538
00:43:07.261 --> 00:43:09.972
Yeah, she's, uh... something, all right.

539
00:43:12.182 --> 00:43:14.226
No, seriously...

540
00:43:14.309 --> 00:43:18.354
How many girls from Kansas end up working
at the Ford Foundation in Indonesia?

541
00:43:19.315 --> 00:43:22.483
Well...
first, you marry an Indonesian guy.

542
00:43:22.567 --> 00:43:27.071
Then you... move to Indonesia
and, uh, realize you need a job.

543
00:43:29.992 --> 00:43:31.327
That's not fair.

544
00:43:33.536 --> 00:43:36.206
Yeah. No... no, it's not.

545
00:43:42.337 --> 00:43:44.297
What are you gonna do with her tomorrow?

546
00:43:44.965 --> 00:43:47.675
Most of the rest of the world
is having Thanksgiving.

547
00:43:50.219 --> 00:43:51.554
Yeah, well...

548
00:43:52.139 --> 00:43:54.058
I'm sure she'll have some ideas.

549
00:44:54.451 --> 00:44:56.245
Is something wrong, honey?

550
00:44:57.662 --> 00:44:59.248
No. I'm just thinking about the movie.

551
00:45:00.332 --> 00:45:01.708
About you and the movie.

552
00:45:04.460 --> 00:45:07.588
And what was it you loved so much?
When you first saw it, I mean.

553
00:45:09.674 --> 00:45:10.925
It was beautiful.

554
00:45:12.635 --> 00:45:14.137
It was so exotic.

555
00:45:15.389 --> 00:45:17.182
I'd never seen anything like it.

556
00:45:19.143 --> 00:45:20.144
Like Dad?

557
00:45:28.651 --> 00:45:31.904
-Your dad hasn't written, has he?
-Why?

558
00:45:35.575 --> 00:45:38.120
Well, he's fallen on hard times,
I'm afraid.

559
00:45:39.620 --> 00:45:40.580
What happened?

560
00:45:41.831 --> 00:45:44.876
You know, it's all second-hand,
but he's not working, and...

561
00:45:45.252 --> 00:45:47.296
When he's not working,
he has a tendency to drink.

562
00:45:50.466 --> 00:45:52.092
He invited me to visit.

563
00:45:53.843 --> 00:45:56.180
That is fantastic.

564
00:45:56.263 --> 00:45:58.265
Oh, honey,
there's a whole part of you there

565
00:45:58.347 --> 00:45:59.932
just waiting to be discovered.

566
00:46:00.641 --> 00:46:02.061
This isn't Roots, Mom.

567
00:46:02.144 --> 00:46:04.771
♪ This land was made
For you and me ♪

568
00:46:04.854 --> 00:46:06.398
Okay, now, this takes me back.

569
00:46:06.482 --> 00:46:08.067
♪ This land is your land ♪

570
00:46:08.150 --> 00:46:12.071
♪ This land is my land,
From California... ♪

571
00:46:12.154 --> 00:46:14.739
-Right on, brother.
- ♪ To the New York island ♪

572
00:46:14.822 --> 00:46:16.533
That is so corny, Mom.

573
00:46:16.616 --> 00:46:20.912
♪ From the redwood forests
To the Gulf Stream waters ♪

574
00:46:20.995 --> 00:46:23.831
♪ This land was made for you and me ♪

575
00:46:25.375 --> 00:46:27.043
How are you, Barry?

576
00:46:28.127 --> 00:46:29.253
How are you, really?

577
00:46:31.548 --> 00:46:32.924
-Honestly?
-Yeah.

578
00:46:35.051 --> 00:46:36.386
I don't know.

579
00:46:37.762 --> 00:46:39.680
Well, sometimes, I feel like I wanna...

580
00:46:40.473 --> 00:46:42.183
breathe in everything
New York has to offer.

581
00:46:43.226 --> 00:46:44.727
Just make it all mine.

582
00:46:45.853 --> 00:46:51.110
Just... And other times,
it all seems like one... big distraction.

583
00:46:52.735 --> 00:46:55.613
People seem like distractions.

584
00:46:56.614 --> 00:46:58.199
I feel like I could... just...

585
00:46:59.617 --> 00:47:01.203
stop talking and...

586
00:47:01.994 --> 00:47:03.122
I don't know, just...

587
00:47:04.831 --> 00:47:05.456
disappear.

588
00:47:07.416 --> 00:47:10.711
And become a monk.
And, uh, read and write, and...

589
00:47:11.254 --> 00:47:12.171
think for a year.

590
00:47:13.382 --> 00:47:14.591
Don't do that.

591
00:47:16.342 --> 00:47:18.928
You know, every time
I open my mouth in class, it's like...

592
00:47:20.096 --> 00:47:22.932
I'm supposed to speak
on behalf of all Black people.

593
00:47:24.684 --> 00:47:26.603
Meanwhile, I, uh...

594
00:47:27.770 --> 00:47:29.481
I quit going
to Black Student Union meetings

595
00:47:29.564 --> 00:47:33.860
because
I didn't feel like I belong there either.

596
00:47:48.250 --> 00:47:50.252
The world is a big place, honey.

597
00:47:51.587 --> 00:47:53.129
You'll find your way.

598
00:48:03.473 --> 00:48:04.558
- Oh.
- Ooh.

599
00:48:04.641 --> 00:48:07.685
Oh... Oh...

600
00:48:08.686 --> 00:48:09.770
Come on, now. Oh!

601
00:48:10.771 --> 00:48:11.772
Oh...

602
00:48:11.856 --> 00:48:15.067
Go up, big man! Go up.

603
00:48:17.236 --> 00:48:20.031
Come on, and let's see. Let's see.

604
00:48:23.117 --> 00:48:24.369
Uh-oh.

605
00:48:24.453 --> 00:48:25.828
-Oh!
-That's a good-ass turn.

606
00:48:25.912 --> 00:48:29.749
Ladies and gentlemen,
young Ralph Ellison in the house tonight!

607
00:48:29.832 --> 00:48:31.959
I know you can't see him.

608
00:48:32.043 --> 00:48:34.086
Uncover your eyes, he already invisible.

609
00:48:37.507 --> 00:48:38.841
- Oh!
- Oh!

610
00:48:38.925 --> 00:48:40.009
Oh!

611
00:48:41.177 --> 00:48:42.011
Oh, my goodness.

612
00:48:42.094 --> 00:48:43.429
That's what I'm talkin' about, baby!

613
00:48:44.013 --> 00:48:46.390
That's the reason
they call him the Invisible Man.

614
00:48:48.602 --> 00:48:50.229
Okay.

615
00:48:51.187 --> 00:48:53.940
Let me give you
some real-deal Saleem advice.

616
00:48:54.023 --> 00:48:56.443
You want to impress her parents?

617
00:48:56.525 --> 00:49:00.279
Order for her. Don't ask her
what she wants, just, um...

618
00:49:00.364 --> 00:49:04.825
I'll have the lobster
and a magnum of your finest champagne.

619
00:49:04.909 --> 00:49:09.706
And the lady will have, uh... a salad,
with a glass of water... with lemon.

620
00:49:09.789 --> 00:49:14.628
Or... how about
whatever they ask you,

621
00:49:14.710 --> 00:49:18.381
you start every answer
with, um, "Shit, motherfucker."

622
00:49:18.881 --> 00:49:24.762
So... "Barry, New York must be
quite the change from Hawaii."

623
00:49:25.722 --> 00:49:28.433
"Shit, motherfucker, it sure is!"

624
00:49:30.559 --> 00:49:36.065
" Have you started to think
about the summer yet, Barry?"

625
00:49:36.148 --> 00:49:41.821
"Shit, motherfucker. I'm considering
taking an internship at a law firm."

626
00:49:41.904 --> 00:49:43.698
These are, uh... They're all great ideas.

627
00:49:45.617 --> 00:49:47.619
-Damn it.
-Here, here. Let me.

628
00:49:47.703 --> 00:49:48.662
Yeah.

629
00:49:48.745 --> 00:49:52.374
You know, there's a lot about me
that you don't know, my brother.

630
00:49:52.456 --> 00:49:56.711
For example, I used to work at a bank
for almost two years.

631
00:49:57.712 --> 00:49:59.005
And I never, ever--

632
00:49:59.088 --> 00:50:00.214
Watch your chin.

633
00:50:00.298 --> 00:50:02.842
Robbed the place. There.

634
00:50:04.719 --> 00:50:08.639
- Another beer for Daddy.
-Thanks.

635
00:50:52.391 --> 00:50:54.060
Would you hand me a towel, please?

636
00:50:56.187 --> 00:50:57.814
-Here you go.
-Thank you.

637
00:51:32.599 --> 00:51:34.518
Oh, there he is.

638
00:51:38.647 --> 00:51:41.691
-Bill Baughman. Pleasure to meet you.
-Uh, Barry.

639
00:51:41.775 --> 00:51:44.193
-My wife, Kathy.
-It's so nice to meet you.

640
00:51:44.276 --> 00:51:46.320
-We've heard so much about you.
-It's a pleasure to meet you.

641
00:51:46.404 --> 00:51:47.405
Thank you.

642
00:51:49.365 --> 00:51:51.535
Barry, I feel we're at a little bit
of a disadvantage

643
00:51:51.618 --> 00:51:53.911
because Charlotte got to spend
the whole day with your mother

644
00:51:53.994 --> 00:51:55.497
and all we get with you is a dinner.

645
00:51:57.833 --> 00:52:00.710
Also, Barry's mother is way cooler
than you two could ever be.

646
00:52:00.794 --> 00:52:02.587
Oh, I don't doubt it.

647
00:52:02.671 --> 00:52:05.047
Well, I'm prepared to make
the most of the time we do have.

648
00:52:05.631 --> 00:52:07.591
I mean, I'll eat two entrées,
if I have to.

649
00:52:09.927 --> 00:52:13.640
You, um... You know,
I have to admit, it's, um...

650
00:52:13.723 --> 00:52:15.975
It's kinda strange for me
to be at the Yale Club.

651
00:52:20.020 --> 00:52:21.147
I'm from a Harvard family.

652
00:52:21.230 --> 00:52:22.774
-Ha.

653
00:52:24.066 --> 00:52:26.570
-I didn't know that.
-That... makes two of us?

654
00:52:26.653 --> 00:52:30.281
Yes, sir. My, uh, father did
his Ph.D. in Economics there,

655
00:52:30.364 --> 00:52:32.534
on a full scholarship,
as a matter of fact.

656
00:52:32.617 --> 00:52:35.119
-Well, he must be brilliant.
-Oh, he is something.

657
00:52:35.912 --> 00:52:37.539
I mean, Dad was born in Kenya.

658
00:52:38.122 --> 00:52:39.415
And he and my mother met

659
00:52:39.499 --> 00:52:41.918
when he was studying
at the University of Hawaii.

660
00:52:42.001 --> 00:52:43.837
The program
that brought him over was, uh...

661
00:52:43.920 --> 00:52:46.005
was funded by the likes of, uh...

662
00:52:46.088 --> 00:52:49.216
Harry Belafonte and Sidney Poitier,
uh, and the Kennedys.

663
00:52:49.300 --> 00:52:52.303
How remarkable. And, uh, where is he now?

664
00:52:52.386 --> 00:52:54.514
Uh... Dad's back in Nairobi.

665
00:52:54.598 --> 00:52:57.934
I believe his title
is Senior Government Economist.

666
00:52:58.685 --> 00:53:00.770
-Hmm.
- Such a fascinating country.

667
00:53:00.854 --> 00:53:02.188
Are you able to visit often?

668
00:53:02.271 --> 00:53:04.774
I plan on going soon.
Well, as soon as, uh...

669
00:53:04.858 --> 00:53:07.486
Charlotte teaches me
a little more Swahili.

670
00:53:07.569 --> 00:53:11.490
Oh... after I finish teaching him
about New York City politics.

671
00:53:11.573 --> 00:53:14.534
-That could take a while.
-And some alcohol.

672
00:53:14.618 --> 00:53:18.204
Well, uh... I hear you're something
of a political organizer, Mr. Baughman.

673
00:53:18.287 --> 00:53:21.332
Oh, no. I wouldn't say that.

674
00:53:22.291 --> 00:53:26.755
I've arranged bail for some organizers.
Represented the unions for years.

675
00:53:26.838 --> 00:53:29.883
But nowadays, I spend more time
raising money for candidates.

676
00:53:29.965 --> 00:53:33.636
It's not something
I particularly enjoy, but...

677
00:53:34.637 --> 00:53:36.180
it's more important than ever.

678
00:53:36.263 --> 00:53:40.852
Pretty soon, our only real game plan
might be to outspend the bad guys.

679
00:53:40.936 --> 00:53:45.105
Oh, and, uh, hope all that money
doesn't turn everybody into bad guys. Hmm.

680
00:53:45.189 --> 00:53:47.943
-Something like that.
- Oh, come on, Dad.

681
00:53:48.025 --> 00:53:49.569
I've seen those pictures of you

682
00:53:49.652 --> 00:53:52.279
and your ponytail
marching for civil rights.

683
00:53:52.780 --> 00:53:55.950
-Sure, but everybody did that.
-Oh, not everybody.

684
00:53:56.283 --> 00:53:59.995
Of course, not everybody.
But everybody we knew in our little...

685
00:54:00.371 --> 00:54:02.122
liberal enclave.

686
00:54:02.331 --> 00:54:03.959
So, how'd your, uh, parents...

687
00:54:04.041 --> 00:54:06.628
feel about your, uh, you know,
"little liberal enclave"?

688
00:54:08.754 --> 00:54:11.799
Mine... not so good. Bill's...

689
00:54:12.299 --> 00:54:13.759
Worse.

690
00:54:13.843 --> 00:54:18.557
But, you know, without their support,
I don't know if I'd have had the courage

691
00:54:18.639 --> 00:54:20.724
to spend my career
fighting the good fight.

692
00:54:21.725 --> 00:54:23.727
I might've gone corporate. Who knows?

693
00:54:24.228 --> 00:54:26.522
Now, I've got a good friend
who's making that decision right now.

694
00:54:27.398 --> 00:54:30.526
He's from the Grant Houses,
if you, uh, know where those are.

695
00:54:31.987 --> 00:54:35.114
-No, I don't believe so. No.
-Harlem.

696
00:54:35.197 --> 00:54:38.492
It's one of the biggest
public housing projects in the city.

697
00:54:38.576 --> 00:54:43.205
Now, he says he, um, didn't make it
to Columbia not to earn money.

698
00:54:43.707 --> 00:54:45.625
Well, that's very understandable.

699
00:54:46.458 --> 00:54:50.714
Yeah. I don't know. It's, um...
I think he's gonna be miserable.

700
00:54:52.006 --> 00:54:52.923
Mm.

701
00:54:56.385 --> 00:54:58.888
But, hey...  But what do I know?

702
00:54:59.471 --> 00:55:04.226
Aside from that fact
that these lamb chops sound amazing.

703
00:55:04.310 --> 00:55:06.771
Mm, well, I couldn't agree more.

704
00:55:11.233 --> 00:55:15.614
-Can we give you a lift back to school?
-Uh, no, thanks. We're going downtown.

705
00:55:15.697 --> 00:55:19.993
-Oh, we are?
-Yes. You... need to dance.

706
00:56:03.036 --> 00:56:04.370
-You think--
-You think you need to teach me.

707
00:56:04.453 --> 00:56:06.246
-I need to teach you?
- Are you kidding?

708
00:56:06.330 --> 00:56:08.290
-That girl was positively afraid of you.
-I wasn't...

709
00:56:08.374 --> 00:56:11.460
Hey, good evening
to you two lovely lovebirds.

710
00:56:11.543 --> 00:56:12.586
Y'all had a good night?

711
00:56:12.671 --> 00:56:13.713
-Mm-hm.
-Great night.

712
00:56:14.338 --> 00:56:15.547
You got a square, college?

713
00:56:16.049 --> 00:56:19.510
-Uh, it's my last one, boss.
-Come on, give me a bust-down, then.

714
00:56:19.802 --> 00:56:20.469
Okay.

715
00:56:24.348 --> 00:56:28.812
Keep it. One of these days,
I'ma make you buy me a pack.

716
00:56:28.894 --> 00:56:31.230
- Whoa. Whoa, whoa, whoa.

717
00:56:31.731 --> 00:56:33.273
What you just try to say to my mans?

718
00:56:34.066 --> 00:56:35.359
You gonna make him?

719
00:56:35.735 --> 00:56:37.319
-Who the fuck is you, nigga?

720
00:56:37.403 --> 00:56:38.654
Coño! Relax, Milton!

721
00:56:38.738 --> 00:56:40.782
Now, who the fuck said
you could walk down my block?

722
00:56:40.865 --> 00:56:43.159
Yo! This kid is cool, Milt.

723
00:56:43.242 --> 00:56:44.618
-Chill out, bro.
-He don't look cool to me.

724
00:56:44.702 --> 00:56:47.538
-Nigga look like a motherfuckin' pussy.
-Man, I live here.

725
00:56:48.915 --> 00:56:51.583
Fuck you. You... ain't from here.

726
00:56:51.667 --> 00:56:54.503
I didn't say I was from here.
I said I live here, okay?

727
00:56:54.586 --> 00:56:56.505
-One forty-two.
-I'm from here!

728
00:56:57.006 --> 00:57:00.969
-You understand? Born and bred.
-Okay, that's great.

729
00:57:01.052 --> 00:57:03.554
-Good for you.
-The fuck is you're walking away from me?

730
